Python has a set of built-in methods that you can use on strings.

#NOTE:-Related methods
Method	Example	Output
upper()	"Hello".upper()	"HELLO"
lower()	"Hello".lower()	"hello"
capitalize()	"hello world".capitalize()	"Hello world"
title()	"hello world".title()	"Hello World"
swapcase()	"Hello".swapcase()	"hELLO"

(1) Upper Case:-
The upper() method, which returns a new string with all alphabetic characters converted to uppercase But
Non-alphabetic characters (like commas, spaces, exclamation marks, numbers) remain unchanged.

###Example;
The upper() method returns the string in upper case:
code:-
a = "Hello, World!"
print(a.upper())
Output:- HELLO, WORLD!

###Example combining with slicing;
python code:-
b = "Hello, World!"
print(b[:5].upper())    #Output:- "HELLO"  (slice then uppercase)
print(b[7:].lower())    #Output:- "world!" (slice then lowercase)

(2) Lower Case:-
The lower() method, which returns a new string with all alphabetic characters converted to lowercase.But
Non-alphabetic characters (commas, spaces, punctuation, numbers) stay the same.

###Example;
The lower() method returns the string in lower case:
code:-
a = "Hello, World!"
print(a.lower())
Output:- hello, world!

###Example combining with slicing;
Code:-
b = "Hello, World!"
print(b[7:].lower())    #Output:- "world!" (slice then lowercase)

###use cases Examples;
python code:-
# Case‑insensitive comparison
user_input = "YES"
if user_input.lower() == "yes":
    print("Confirmed")   #Output:- "Confirmed"
# Cleaning text for processing
text = "Python Is FUN!"
clean_text = text.lower()   #Output:- "python is fun!"

(3) Remove Whitespace:-the strip() method, which removes leading and trailing whitespace
(spaces, tabs, newlines, etc.) from a string.
Whitespace is the space before and/or after the actual text, and very often you want to remove this space.

###Example;
The strip() method removes any whitespace from the beginning or the end:

a = " Hello, World! "  #here is space before H and after d. so it remove space
print(a.strip())       {The space before "Hello" and the space after "!" are removed.
                          Whitespace inside the string (between words) is preserved.}
Output:-"Hello, World!"

#NOTE:-Related methods
Method	Effect	Example	Output
strip()	Removes whitespace from both ends	" hello ".strip()	"hello"
lstrip()	Removes whitespace only from the left (start)	" hello ".lstrip()	"hello "
rstrip()	Removes whitespace only from the right (end)	" hello ".rstrip()	" hello"

(4) Replace String:-The replace() method, which returns a new string where all occurrences 
of a specified substring are replaced with another substring.

###Example;
The replace() method replaces a string with another string:
Code:-
a = "Hello, World!"         # "H" is replaced with "J" → "Jello, World!"
                            The original string a remains unchanged (strings are immutable).
print(a.replace("H", "J"))
Output:- "Jello, World!"

### Python Syntax:- string.replace(old, new, count)
old – substring to be replaced
new – substring to replace with
count (optional) – number of occurrences to replace (default: all)

###More examples;
python code:-
text = "one one was a race horse, two two was one too."         ## Replace all occurrences
print(text.replace("one", "three"))  
Output:- "three three was a race horse, two two was three too."

print(text.replace("one", "three", 2))                         ## Replace only first 2 occurrences
Output:- "three three was a race horse, two two was one too."

print(text.replace("one", "X").replace("two", "Y"))           ## Replace multiple different substrings (chain)
Output:- "X X was a race horse, Y Y was X too."

(5) Split String:-The split() method, which divides a string into a list of substrings
based on a specified separator.
The split() method returns a list where the text between the specified separator
becomes the list items.

###Example;
The split() method splits the string into substrings if it finds instances of the separator.
code:-
a = "Hello, World!"      #The separator "," tells Python to cut the string wherever a comma appears.
                          The result is a list with two items: "Hello" and " World!"
                          (note the space before World! because the split keeps the rest of the characters).
print(a.split(","))                  
Output:- ['Hello', ' World!']

###More examples;
text = "apple,banana,cherry"
print(text.split(","))     #Output:- ['apple', 'banana', 'cherry']

# Default separator is any whitespace (space, tab, newline)
sentence = "Hello world Python"
print(sentence.split())    #Output:- ['Hello', 'world', 'Python']

# Limit the number of splits using maxsplit parameter
data = "one,two,three,four"
print(data.split(",", 2))  #Output:- ['one', 'two', 'three,four']

#IMPORTANT NOTE:- String method is just Opposite method ofjoin()
python code:-
items = ['Hello', 'World!']
print(" ".join(items))     #Output 'Hello World!'




















































